# §0. What this contributes

01_CORE §11 proves the exact no-go: at cubic order the third-station Hessian
determinant is a negative multiple of a sum of squares, so a third *maximum* in
the value window can only be created by quartic remainders **near the cubic
degeneracy set**. §14 item 2 flags the missing piece: uniform finite-r control
of that boundary layer. This file supplies the first tracked component:

**The degeneracy bracket is affine in the window height α. Therefore the set of
window heights at which near-degeneracy can occur is an interval whose length
is exactly computable — and the "localization near the degeneracy sets" becomes
a measure estimate with explicit constants, uniformly on the generic and
transverse charts.**

# §1. Three equivalent forms of the bracket, and the affine one is the tool

Generic chart (cs ≠ 0), K = −4c² − 4cη − η² + 2s²w. The manuscript's §11 box:

g(α) := (K + 8αcη)² + 64α(1−α)c²η².

Expand: K² + 16αcηK + 64α²c²η² + 64αc²η² − 64α²c²η² = **K² + 16αcηK + 64αc²η²**.

Convex form (AI edit 0003 Note 1): (1−α)K² + α(K+8cη)²
= K² + α[(K+8cη)² − K²] = K² + α[16cηK + 64c²η²] = **K² + 16αcηK + 64αc²η²**. ✓

Affine form (new, and the working one):

\[
\boxed{\;g(\alpha)=K^2+8\alpha c\eta\,(2K+8c\eta)\;}
\qquad
g(0)=K^2,\quad g(1)=(K+8c\eta)^2,\quad
g'(\alpha)=8c\eta(2K+8c\eta).
\]

All three verified equal by the displayed expansions. (Provenance note: the
convex form remains a same-line result — this is my third derivation of it, and
it still awaits the 30-second cross-line check flagged since DER-004; nothing
below depends on which of the three forms is taken as primary, since the
equality is shown.)

**Transverse chart (c = 0, s = 1)** — the manuscript's second box has the
*identical structure*: with A = a+η, B = a−η,

\[
(a+(1-2\alpha)\eta)^2+4\alpha(1-\alpha)\eta^2
=(1-\alpha)(a+\eta)^2+\alpha(a-\eta)^2
=A^2+\alpha(B^2-A^2),
\]

verified by expanding: a² + 2aη(1−2α) + η² on both sides. ✓ Affine in α with
slope B² − A² = −4aη. **One lemma will therefore serve both charts.**

# §2. The window-measure lemma (exact)

**Lemma.** Let g(α) = A² + α(B²−A²) on α ∈ [0,1], A,B real. For ε ≥ 0:

\[
\mathrm{Leb}\{\alpha\in[0,1]:g(\alpha)\le\varepsilon\}
\;\le\;
\min\Bigl\{1,\ \frac{\varepsilon}{|B^2-A^2|}\Bigr\},
\]

and the set is **empty** when min(A², B²) > ε.

*Proof.* g is affine with endpoint values A², B², both ≥ 0, so g ≥ min(A²,B²)
on [0,1] — giving emptiness — and the sublevel set is the intersection of [0,1]
with a half-line whose boundary moves at speed |g′| = |B²−A²|; an affine
function spends length ≤ ε/|g′| below any level ε above its minimum endpoint
when the other endpoint exceeds ε... more simply: {g ≤ ε} is an interval with
g varying linearly across it, so its length is (ε − min g)₊/|g′| ≤ ε/|B²−A²|. ∎

Chart dictionary: generic chart |B²−A²| = 8|cη||2K+8cη|; transverse chart
|B²−A²| = 4|a|η.

# §3. Finite-t inclusion: the maximum event forces g small

At physical radius t with η = r/t, the third-station constraints on the full
field determine the cubic (a,Q) as their exact-cubic values plus O(t)·jets
(quartic feedback through the constraint solve), and K involves only
(c,s,η,w) — **K is untouched by the constraint shift.** Writing the physical
Hessian as t·D²P + O(t²)𝒫(J) and det H_x = t²det D²P + O(t³)𝒫(J), with
∂det/∂(a,Q) bounded by 𝒫(J)/(c²s²) on the generic chart:

\[
\{H_x\prec0\}\ \Rightarrow\ \det H_x>0\ \Rightarrow\
\frac{\eta^2}{64c^2s^2}\,g(\alpha)\le C_0\,t\,\mathcal P(J)\Bigl(1+\frac1{c^2s^2}\Bigr)
\ \Rightarrow\
\boxed{\;g(\alpha)\le C_1\,\frac{t^3}{r^2}\,\mathcal P(J)\;}
\]

using c²s² ≤ ¼ and η² = r²/t². The transverse-chart version is identical with
the milder prefactor 4/η² and pole only in the a-direction slope.

# §4. The two-regime bound, constants tracked (generic chart)

The height-window integral of the maximum indicator, at fixed direction (c,s)
and radius t, is ℓ × (α-measure). By §2–§3:

\[
\int \mathbf 1_{\{H_x\prec0\}}\,du
\;\le\;
\frac{r^3}{6}\,
\min\Bigl\{1,\ \frac{C_1t^3\mathcal P(J)}{8r^2|c\eta|\,|2K+8c\eta|}\Bigr\}
=\frac{r^3}{6}\,
\min\Bigl\{1,\ \frac{C_1t^4\mathcal P(J)}{8r^3|c|\,|2K+8c\eta|}\Bigr\}.
\]

Split on the slope: **Regime I** (|2K+8cη| ≥ κ and |c| ≥ c₀): the second branch
gives an extra factor C₁t⁴𝒫/(8κc₀r³) beyond the window width — the
localization gain, polynomial in jets, explicit. **Regime II**
(|2K+8cη| < κ): use branch 1 (measure ≤ 1) and pay instead the *probability*
of the thin slab. Since 2K+8cη is affine in w with coefficient 4s², and w has
conditional density bounded by 1/√(4π) (variance 2 under the closed nine-jet
law, GP-CLS-006):

\[
P\bigl(|2K+8c\eta|<\kappa\bigr)\;\le\;\frac{\kappa}{4s^2\sqrt\pi}
\;\le\;\frac{\kappa}{4s_0^2\sqrt\pi}
\quad(s\ge s_0\text{ on the generic chart}).
\]

Choosing κ = t² balances the regimes: both contribute
≤ C₂(c₀,s₀)·r³·t²·𝒫-moments per unit t, and \(\int_{cr}^{\delta}t^2\,dt\)
is bounded — the singular-region maximum count on the generic chart is
**O(r³) with every constant named**: C₁ (the crude det-derivative envelope),
1/(4s₀²√π) (the small-ball constant), sixth-order Gaussian moments of 𝒫
(finite by GP-CLS-006's closed law). The transverse chart runs the same two
regimes with slope 4|a|η and small-ball in a (density ≤ 1/√(4π), same law),
poles in c absent by construction.

